WEBVTT

00:00.570 --> 00:04.721
A scheduler prepares a compatible JSON event saying the job completed.

00:04.721 --> 00:07.481
The catalogue and investigator need that history.

00:07.481 --> 00:09.643
Does the document alone establish a working

00:09.643 --> 00:12.153
connection, or make the record retrievable?

00:12.153 --> 00:15.404
Pause and name what else the producer and receiver must agree.

00:20.530 --> 00:24.553
A configured integration must observe the execution and emit events.

00:24.553 --> 00:27.560
Both ends need compatible transport, endpoint

00:27.560 --> 00:30.192
and authentication, with submission permission.

00:30.192 --> 00:33.403
Even a successful HTTP acknowledgement is not

00:33.403 --> 00:35.798
proof that the record is already queryable.

00:35.798 --> 00:37.970
Check errors, retries and gaps.

00:37.970 --> 00:42.275
Neither the event schema nor HTTP guarantees exactly-once delivery.

00:43.292 --> 00:45.857
Now suppose another scheduler also has a job called

00:45.857 --> 00:50.015
build_delivery_summary and a local execution label R1205.

00:50.015 --> 00:55.055
If both send those short names, can the receiver reliably identify which execution

00:55.055 --> 00:56.672
belongs to which system?

00:56.672 --> 00:59.750
Explain what identity information is missing before continuing.

01:04.670 --> 01:08.026
Use a namespaced job identity and a run UUID.

01:08.026 --> 01:12.748
The page explicitly maps its short R1205 label to the exchange UUID.

01:12.748 --> 01:15.747
Reuse that UUID for events in the same run

01:15.747 --> 01:18.311
cycle; a new execution needs a new identity.

01:18.311 --> 01:22.747
Map the output's namespace and name to the catalogue's local dataset identifier,

01:22.747 --> 01:24.344
retaining the correspondence.

01:24.344 --> 01:27.165
A matching table name cannot infer those mappings.

01:28.130 --> 01:30.638
Read the event as a sourced completion claim.

01:30.638 --> 01:36.098
It names input and output datasets, but does not preserve the retained input snapshot,

01:36.098 --> 01:39.301
code version, output version or report refresh.

01:39.301 --> 01:41.142
Those need additional records.

01:41.142 --> 01:45.788
OpenLineage supplies an event model; CloudEvents supplies a distinct general envelope.

01:45.788 --> 01:51.415
The page's catalogue query API is illustrative, not an OpenLineage retrieval standard.

01:51.415 --> 01:53.962
Read permission is separate from permission to submit.

01:54.757 --> 01:56.476
Try the page's map exercise.

01:56.476 --> 01:59.286
In Run's Example record, find the run identifier

01:59.286 --> 02:02.401
and job name, then follow Job to compare the name.

02:02.401 --> 02:07.856
Look for the job namespace and exchange UUID: this map example does not contain them.

02:07.856 --> 02:12.005
Explain which mappings the receiver still needs, then use Check your answer.

02:12.005 --> 02:14.692
The page keeps the full event and connection example;

02:14.692 --> 02:16.922
its example addresses are not running services.
